To set up a UART connection on the STM32F429ZI (such as on a Nucleo-144 board) using STM32CubeIDE, use the onboard ST-LINK Virtual COM Port (tied to USART3 or USART2 depending on board routing) or configure a custom USART/UART instance.

1. Hardware Connection

• Onboard USB (Virtual COM Port): The STM32F429ZI Nucleo board routes USART3 (PD8 for TX, PD9 for RX) or USART2 (PA2 for TX, PA3 for RX) to the onboard ST-LINK debugger, which bridges directly to your PC via USB.
• External UART / USB-to-TTL Converter: If using an external module, cross-connect the pins:
	• Connect MCU TX to Adapter RX.
	• Connect MCU RX to Adapter TX.
	• Share a common GND between both devices.

2. CubeMX / CubeIDE Configuration

1. Open your project's .ioc file in STM32CubeIDE.
2. In the Pinout & Configuration tab, expand Connectivity.
3. Select your USART instance (e.g., USART3 or USART2).
4. Set the Mode to Asynchronous.
5. Verify and adjust the Parameter Settings:
	• Baud Rate: 115200 bits/sec (or 9600 depending on your terminal preference)
	• Word Length: 8 Bits (7 data bits + 1 parity, or standard 8 data bits)
	• Parity: None
	• Stop Bits: 1
	• Direction: Transmit and Receive
6. (Optional) In the NVIC Settings tab, check the box for USART global interrupt if you plan to use interrupt-driven or DMA communication.
7. Press Ctrl + S to save and let CubeIDE generate the initialization code.

3. Writing the Code (main.c)

Add transmission or reception code inside your main.c file using the HAL library:

Transmitting a String (Polling Mode)

c
/* USER CODE BEGIN Includes */
#include <string.h>
/* USER CODE END Includes */

// Inside main() after initialization:
char msg[] = "Hello STM32F429ZI UART!\r\n";

while (1)
{
  HAL_UART_Transmit(&huart3, (uint8_t*)msg, strlen(msg), HAL_MAX_DELAY);
  HAL_Delay(1000);
}
Use code with caution.

Receiving Data

c
uint8_t rxData;

// To receive a single byte:
HAL_UART_Receive(&huart3, &rxData, 1, HAL_MAX_DELAY);
Use code with caution.

4. Testing on Your Computer

1. Connect the USB cable from the board to your PC.
2. Open a serial terminal program (such as PuTTY, Tera Term, or the built-in serial monitor in STM32CubeIDE).
3. Select the correct COM port assigned to the ST-LINK Virtual COM Port and match the baud rate (e.g., 115200).
